@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the warning inside the prescription, examined
#
# The statement at pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription stood at the head
# of queries/untested-generalizations.rq with four witnesses. Its own note named
# the falsifier and the first place to look for it, the tantric material and its
# commentarial layer. This protocol reads every caution in the holding against
# the statement, opens the tantric case at a second work, and opens the one case
# the reading turned up in which the warning stands at a later station.
#
# The searches carried for it are in
# examinations/searches-self-warning-inside-the-prescription.ttl.
#

pmp:SelfWarningInsideThePrescriptionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the warning against valuing a practice wrongly stands in the prescribing passage, or arrives later"@en, "Ob die Warnung davor, eine Praxis falsch zu schätzen, in der vorschreibenden Stelle steht oder später hinzukommt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission prescribes a practice the practitioner is liable to value for the wrong reason, the warning against that valuing stands inside the prescribing passage, in the same source, and is not supplied by a later commentator or an outside critic. The population is every pm:Cautioning node in the holding on main on 2026-10-21, 296 of them. Drawn in two passes: a keyword scan of label and definition for attachment, pride, vanity, value, esteem, obstacle, impediment, delight, pleasure, gold, wealth, display, marvel, greed, lust and their neighbours, which returned 61 nodes, every one read at the node; and a reading of all 296 labels, which added twelve candidates the scan had missed, every one read at the node. Two works were opened where the node could not settle the case: the Kulārṇava Tantra for the tantric witness and the Rasārṇava for the mercurial one."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "As the statement names it: a transmission prescribes such a practice, its earliest witness carries no warning against the practitioner's own valuing of it, and the warning is demonstrably first added by a commentator or a later recension. Met in part, once: in the mercurial transmission the classical work read offers the end of poverty as a fruit of its metallic operations and carries no warning against making gold for gain in any place a term search reached, and the restriction appears in a compilation of 1926 as the compiler's footnote. That the compiler or anyone after the classical work first added it is not shown; the works between were not opened and the compiler ascribes the practice to living ascetics."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:baseRate "Of the 296 cautions, eighteen warn a practitioner against valuing wrongly something a practice gives or is. Placed against the statement: eight stand inside the prescribing passage of their own source, one of them the statement's own witness Zosimos and seven from six transmissions not among its four; three stand in the same source at another place; one stands at a later station of the same transmission; one is supplied by a commentary to a base text that prescribes no practice for the thing warned about; five are near edges, listed in a note. With the statement's four and the new tantric place, ten transmissions carry the device at the narrow reading and one departs from it."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched: the 296 pm:Cautioning nodes of the compiled holding on 2026-10-21, by the two passes in the definition; the Kulārṇava Tantra, ullāsa 2, in two printings and Rai's translation; the Rasārṇava, by a whole-text term search in the Sanskrit of the Bibliotheca Indica edition; the Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, pp. xiii and 337, at the wording. Found for the statement: six transmissions whose cautions meet it at the narrow reading and were not among its witnesses, and an earlier-standing place for the tantric caution it rests on. Found against it: the mercurial case. Not searched: works the holding has not opened, and cautions coded as anything other than pm:Cautioning; a warning of this shape entered as a pm:Conceptualizing or pm:Originating node, as three of the statement's four witnesses are, is outside the population."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-21" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-21" ;
    skos:note "The candidate the statement named holds. Its tantric witness was the Mahānirvāṇa, and the statement asked whether a caution there belongs to the base text or to its readers. Another work of the same tradition, the Kulārṇava, carries the warning in its second ullāsa in the god's own mouth, between the verse that sets wine and flesh as of the greatest fruit for the Kaula and the verses that close the matter with the rule: if drinking alone gave accomplishment, every drunkard would have it; drinking to no purpose is the great sin; taken outside the rule they destroy one's knowledge (pmc:KularnavaMadyapanaMatrena). Prescription and caution are one passage and one speaker. Whichever of the two tantras is the earlier, the caution is not an addition peculiar to the later one."@en ;
    skos:note "The six transmissions the reading added at the narrow reading, each with the place. Pātañjala yoga: the powers are obstacles to samādhi, said among the sūtras that teach how each power is had (pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle). Theravāda: luminosity, rapture and the rest arise in the right practitioner at the start of insight and become the imperfections when taken as proof of attainment, in the exposition that leads the practitioner to them (pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight). Jain: the text warns against the very marks it prescribes, without withdrawing them (pmc:JainaCautionAgainstOutwardTokens). Japanese Zen: the preface that promises long life from the inner contemplation calls the man who keeps it for that a corpse-guarding ghost (pmc:JapaneseZenShushiki). Thelema: the instruction warns before all its practices against attributing objective reality to any of the results, and opens its last chapter by saying its own previous experiment has little value (pmc:ThelemaCautioContraRealitatem, pmc:ThelemaCautioContraSuamPraescriptionem). Celestial Masters: the admonitions that require a hundred practices and a thousand good deeds discard the whole merit of the man moved by profit in the middle of the work (pmc:CmZhongweiliDong)."@en ;
    skos:note "What the second of these settles. The statement's note recorded the Theravāda handbook as a near miss on every count: two self-warnings of the wrong content inside the prescribing passage and one of the right content outside it. The imperfections of insight are of the right content and stand inside the passage, and were already in the holding; the note read the handbook at three nodes and not at the fourth. The decision the note left open — whether the prescribing passage means the passage or the same source — is no longer forced by the count: at the narrow reading the statement now has ten transmissions, and the wider one is not needed to keep it."@en ;
    skos:note "The case that departs, and why it departs on a word the statement did not decide. The Rasa-Jala-Nidhi forbids the making of gold except for bare subsistence in a footnote of its own, set in the middle of its sixteen gold processes, which is the statement's shape in a compilation of 1926 (pmc:RasaSvarnakaranaNisedha); its preface ascribes the restraint to the ascetic chemists of the compiler's own time. The Rasārṇava, the classical work of the same transmission the holding has opened, prescribes the piercing of metals, says that mercury acts on metal as it acts on the body, and names the destruction of poverty as a fruit beside the destruction of old age (pmc:RasarnavaJaraDaridryaNasana). Where it speaks of greed it speaks of the teacher, who must be free of it, and of the loss of knowledge. In the earliest witness read, gain is not a wrong reason at all. The judgement that the valuing is wrong arrives with the later station, and the warning with it."@en ;
    skos:note "What that does to the statement. Its antecedent, a practice the practitioner is liable to value for the wrong reason, was written as if the wrongness were fixed. The mercurial case shows it can move along a transmission, and where it moves the warning moves with it. The statement can be kept as written only by reading the antecedent as wrong in the eyes of the source that prescribes, and then the mercurial case is no counterexample and the classical work no witness; that reading makes it harder to break and is not adopted here. The deviation is recorded as one case at a later station, with the limit in the falsifier above: the works between the Rasārṇava and 1926 are unopened."@en ;
    skos:note "The near edges, so that a later reading can move them. Hasidism, twice, in a work that permits fasting and mortification rather than prescribing them (pmc:HasidismTaanitEinayim, pmc:HasidismSigufimLeMadrega) — the cautions are of the right content and stand beside the practice. Chan, twice, where the warned valuing is a misreading of the work's own teaching by practitioners of another school (pmc:ChanKongxinJingzuo, pmc:ChanChangzuoTongWuqing). The Liber iuratus, where the warning is against performing the operation at all and not against a valuing of it (pmc:IuratusSpiritusTerreniKautel). Not counted either way: the Sāṃkhya caution on the powers, which the commentary supplies to a base text that names no practice for them and lists no powers (pmc:SamkhyaMohaAisvarya), and the same-source cautions at another place, the Theravāda refusal of display and impediment of the powers and the Abramelin counterfeit (pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused, pmc:IddhiPannayaPalibodha, pmc:AbramelinSignaPseudangeli). Read and set aside as not concerning a valuing of a prescribed practice: Isaac on asking for signs, where no wonder-working is prescribed (pmc:SyriacAtwataDlaAnanqe); John of the Cross on revelations, which the work describes and does not prescribe (pmc:SubidaCautelaRevelationum); the Thelemic warning against the absorbed state, which is against the attainment itself (pmc:ThelemaCautioContraSamadhi); Zhu Xi on quiet sitting, a later teacher on a predecessor's method (pmc:ZhuziZuobing); the Nāth verse on kuṇḍalinī, the Celestial Masters' rule on money and the Jain rule against the arts (pmc:NathKundaliniBandhana, pmc:CmShibuShouqian, pmc:JainaCautionAgainstNimittaAndCikitsa). Cautions by an outside critic were found — Snorri on seiðr, the Spanish report on the Andean fire-readers, the Christian commentary on the Mithraic ordeals — and none of them concerns a valuing."@en ;
    skos:note "How the population was drawn and what that misses. The keyword scan caught 61 cautions and missed one of the statement's own witnesses, Zosimos, which has none of the words; the reading of all labels was added for that reason and found twelve more candidates. A caution whose label does not show its content can still have been missed. The cheapest next question is the one the deviation raises: open a work between the Rasārṇava and the compilation of 1926 — the Rasaratnasamuccaya is named in the holding as an unopened work of this corpus — and see whether the restriction on gold stands there, and whether gain is treated there as a wrong reason."@en .
